Root cause:
The bug is caused by the `free_transition_pgtable` function being called in multiple places during error handling in the `init_transition_pgtable` function and the `machine_kexec_cleanup` function. This leads to a double free of the same memory pages if an error occurs during the initialization of the transition page table, resulting in crashes when memory allocation fails.

Code statements information:
The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
free_transition_pgtable(image);
```
```c
if (!p4d) goto err;
```
```c
if (!pud) goto err;
```
Reason:
The bug occurs because `free_transition_pgtable(image)` is called in the `init_transition_pgtable` function when memory allocation fails (e.g., when `get_zeroed_page` returns NULL). If this happens, the function attempts to free memory that may have already been freed in a previous error handling path, leading to a double free of the same memory pages.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
// Removed the call to free_transition_pgtable(image);
```
```c
image->arch.p4d = NULL;
```
```c
image->arch.pud = NULL;
```
Reason:
The fix involves removing the call to `free_transition_pgtable(image)` from the error handling path in `init_transition_pgtable`, thus preventing the double free. Additionally, setting the pointers (`image->arch.p4d`, `image->arch.pud`, etc.) to NULL after freeing ensures that subsequent calls to free the same memory do not occur, as they will check for NULL before attempting to free.


